const intro={"n":30,"title":"Martín Fierro and the Moreno: A Singing Contest and a Reckoning","nums":[1064,1092,1128,1131],"summary":"Martín Fierro and the Black singer introduced in the preceding canto meet in a sustained sung contest, or contrapunto. They exchange questions and poetic answers about the sounds of heaven, earth, sea and night, the origin of love, the nature of law, and time and measurement. The Moreno challenges unequal treatment and says the law catches the poor like a spiderweb. After the contest, he reveals another reason for approaching Fierro: one of his brothers was killed by a violent man. The sequence makes clear that he is referring to the killing from the first part. The tension escalates, but no further killing occurs in this canto.","people":"Martín Fierro; the Moreno, a skilled Black singer and brother of the man Fierro killed earlier; an audience witnessing the exchange.","themes":"Oral poetry and improvisation; racism and racial dignity; justice and class inequality; unanswered responsibility for violence; intellectual rivalry; reconciliation versus revenge.","words":"contrapunto (sung exchange or competition); payar (to improvise sung verse); moreno (historical term for a Black man); ley (law); redota (defeat, vernacular); guitarra (guitar).","questions":["Which subjects are debated in the singing contest?","How does the Moreno challenge racial hierarchies?","What comparison does he make between the law and a spiderweb?","What new fact about his dead brother does the Moreno reveal?","Why is this confrontation connected to the murder in Part One, Canto VII?"],"analysis":"The Moreno is an articulate interlocutor who explicitly rejects a division of humanity by skin color. The narrator and Fierro nevertheless use racialized expressions that require criticism, not normalization. The singing competition is also a moral reckoning: Fierro's past killing has consequences, and the Moreno's family remembers it. The participants' eventual avoidance of an immediate fight belongs to the following canto.","related":"/en/students/canto-7/","relatedTitle":"The original killing in Part One, Canto VII"};
---
